  "content": {
    "id": "p51-q24-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "24",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x-3}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p51-q24-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "State the domain and range of $f^{-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Domain: $[0,\\infty)$.\n\nRange: $\\left[\\dfrac32,\\infty\\right)$.",
          "worked": "For $f$, $2x-3\\ge0$ gives $x\\ge\\dfrac32$, and the square root has range $[0,\\infty)$. Interchange the domain and range.\n\nDomain of $f^{-1}$: $[0,\\infty)$.\n\nRange of $f^{-1}$: $\\left[\\dfrac32,\\infty\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q24-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $f^{-1}(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+3}{2}$, $x\\ge0$.",
          "worked": "Let $y=\\sqrt{2x-3}$. Swap $x$ and $y$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\sqrt{2y-3}\\\\x^2&=2y-3\\\\2y&=x^2+3\\\\y&=\\frac{x^2+3}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe swapped square-root equation requires $x\\ge0$. Therefore $f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+3}{2}$, with domain $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      }
    ]
  },
